{
  "version": 1,
  "description": "The shared route table. Route ids and deep-link paths are identical on all four platforms; only the platform binding differs. Every route must be reachable by deep link, because automation cannot rely on tapping its way to a screen — see launch-args.json.",
  "rules": [
    "Route ids match screen ids in screens.json. A screen with no route is a component, not a screen.",
    "Deep-link paths are identical across platforms. The scheme is per app (see app-identity.json) — usesmileid-sample-android://products, usesmileid-sample-ios://products, and so on.",
    "Sheets are LAYERS over the screen that owns them, never destinations that replace it. Their paths stay in this table and stay deep-linkable — a link resolves to the owner's own route with the sheet open, so a flow can still drive and assert one. A sheet that replaced the destination beneath it would leave nothing behind its scrim; each app draws a sheet as a njia sheet step over the page that opened it.",
    "The SDK flow is ONE route with TWO presentations. Same destination, different container.",
    "Arguments are typed on every platform. A stringly-typed argument that only fails at runtime is a defect.",
    "Dev-only shell surfaces are NOT routes in this table, deliberately. A component gallery or API scratchpad belongs to one shell, is not part of the journey the four apps share, and must not oblige the other three to build a counterpart. They still get a deep link under their app's own scheme, because reaching one cold is the only way a flow can drive it, and their ids use a dev_ prefix so the sample_ namespace stays the spec's. Android and Flutter have one today, the component gallery: usesmileid-sample-android://debug/components and usesmileid-sample-flutter://debug/components."
  ],
